{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012XwrrRf4DjeTStRGgxpq9H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GXAemmcj4QmGtPdHQQ1SUh","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Temporal Measurement Drift in LLM Benchmarks\n\n**Paper:** Temporal Measurement Drift in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis\n**Venue Target:** EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, complete roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Assignments\n\n- **R1 (Methods + Experiments):** Statistical methodology, experimental controls, reproducibility\n- **R2 (Novelty + Related Work):** Originality, positioning, contribution significance, framing\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n\n### R1: Methods + Experiments\n\n**Summary:** The paper applies standard Mantel–Haenszel DIF with ETS classification to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% of items exhibit large temporal DIF between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. A semi-synthetic injection framework with cross-subject matching validates detection power (ΔTPR up to +0.674, ΔFPR = 0.000). The finding replicates across six benchmarks (17.9–31.3%).\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. **Thorough experimental controls.** Systematic elimination of alternatives: random split (0.18%), ability quintile (2.9%), matched-ability (~28–29%), architecture homogeneity (Jaccard=1.0), permutation null (>600σ). Convergence across methods is convincing.\n2. **Well-executed semi-synthetic validation.** Dose-response curve is clean and monotonic. Cross-subject matching addresses a genuine methodological concern (matching variable contamination) with formal guarantee and empirical boundary analysis.\n3. **Unprecedented scale.** 5,227 models × 6 benchmarks × 27,125 total items is the largest DIF analysis applied to LLM evaluation.\n4. **Ground truth meta-critique is a genuine conceptual contribution.** The global-exposure vs. differential-functioning distinction is well-articulated, with clean empirical support (enrichment=0.964, φ=0.012).\n5. **Statistical rigor.** Dual ETS-C threshold provides implicit multiple-testing control (BH correction changes 31.32% to 31.30%). Permutation null adds formal significance evidence.\n6. **Honest limitation handling.** Acknowledges MH-DIF detects *that* not *why*; correctly labels GSM1K as exploratory; notes additional 24.8% non-uniform DIF from logistic regression.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n1. **(Major) Causal interpretation gap.** The 31% DIF rate conflates contamination, genuine capability improvement, training methodology shifts, and format sensitivity. The Simpson's paradox result shows item properties drive DIF, not accuracy gains — but this doesn't distinguish causes at the item level. The difficulty×direction interaction (ρ = −0.597) suggests capability change but is not developed rigorously. Without causal decomposition, the audit protocol's actionability is unclear: retire items because contaminated, or retain them because they reveal genuine shifts?\n2. **(Major) Semi-synthetic framework is methodologically straightforward.** The injection procedure (incorrect→correct flipping) is standard DIF simulation. Cross-subject matching has psychometric precedent (Nandakumar 1993, Donoghue 1993). Proposition 1 is essentially immediate from the definition. The contribution is the *application context*, not the technique.\n3. **(Major) Non-independence of models.** The 5,227 models include fine-tuned variants, merges, and quantized versions sharing base weights. MH-DIF's validity requires conditional independence given stratification, violated when models share systematic biases. The graduated deduplication (K=10: 22.7%, K=20: 29.1%) is helpful but the permutation test for local dependence tests *item-level* LD, not the *model-level* correlation structure. A permutation shuffling temporal labels while preserving model correlation would be more appropriate.\n4. **(Minor) Temporal cohort definition is coarse.** 6-month window (2023-H2 vs 2024-H1) yields *higher* DIF (34.35%) than full-year — unexpected if temporal distance drives drift. May capture a specific transition (RLHF revolution, MoE adoption) rather than gradual drift.\n5. **(Minor) GSM1K correlation (r = −0.478, N = 33) underpowered.** 95% CI spans [−0.70, −0.16]; partial correlation barely significant (p = 0.027). Over-prominent for its evidential weight.\n6. **(Minor) \"FPR\" terminology in semi-synthetic tables misleading.** Baseline \"FPR\" of 0.314 is actually the genuine temporal DIF flagging rate, not false positives. Should be labeled \"C%\" or \"flagging rate.\"\n7. **(Minor) Architecture confound dismissed too quickly.** Within decoder-only: MoE vs dense, different attention mechanisms, context lengths — all could produce DIF through format sensitivity.\n\n**Questions to Authors:**\n1. Could the matched-ability C% reduction (31.3→28.9%) partly reflect reduced statistical power (N drops from 1,887 to 1,276)?\n2. Are the same model cohorts used across all 6 benchmarks? If so, the 6 DIF analyses share examinees and are not independent.\n3. Does the permutation null shuffle temporal labels while preserving model correlation structure?\n4. Within-family Llama ability matching reduces C% by 33pp vs only 2.4pp for temporal — how do you reconcile both \"converging to ~28–29%\"?\n5. What is the direction distribution among 31% ETS-C items (favoring 2023 vs 2024)?\n6. Proposition 1 guarantees zero ΔFPR under injection. How do you know cross-subject matching eliminates bias in the observational (non-injection) setting?\n\n**R1 Scores:** Soundness 3.5 | Excitement 3.0 | Reproducibility 4.0 | Overall 3.5 | Confidence 4\n\n---\n\n### R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\n**Summary:** The paper imports MH-DIF from educational psychometrics to detect temporal measurement drift in LLM benchmarks, finding 17.9–31.3% instability across six benchmarks. It argues web-overlap contamination labels are orthogonal to DIF flags (enrichment ~1.0), proposes this as a validation methodology critique, and offers a semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching.\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. **Scale and thoroughness.** Largest DIF study on LLM benchmarks. Six-benchmark gradient from knowledge-intensive to procedural is genuinely informative.\n2. **Global-vs-differential distinction is the strongest intellectual contribution.** Clear, useful, and empirically confirmed (enrichment=0.964, φ=0.012). Important for how contamination detection community validates methods.\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation carefully designed.** Zero-FPR guarantee, dose-response curve, multi-subject boundary analysis, GSM8K portability.\n4. **Honest about limitations.** Correctly acknowledges what MH-DIF cannot do.\n5. **Multiple control conditions.** Collectively convincing that 31% is not artifactual.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n1. **(Major) Core method is off-the-shelf with no algorithmic modification.** MH-DIF with ETS classification is unchanged from Holland & Thayer 1988. Novelty depends entirely on findings being surprising or actionable, but rankings preserved (ρ~0.997) undercuts urgency. For main conference, need either (a) methodological contribution beyond standard MH-DIF, or (b) findings leading to concrete actionable changes.\n2. **(Major) Related work gaps.** Missing important citations: Deng et al. (2024) on item-level contamination across benchmarks; Yang et al. (2023) on MMLU contamination via rephrasing; Jacovi et al. (2023) on benchmark lifecycle; Raji et al. (2021) on benchmark misuse. Insufficient engagement with Maeda (2025) despite being the most directly comparable DIF-for-LLM work. Only 7 references in main text creates impression of insularity.\n3. **(Major) Ground truth meta-critique is partially circular.** The argument: DIF and web-overlap are orthogonal → semi-synthetic shows DIF works → therefore ground truth is wrong. But step 2 only shows DIF detects *injected* differential behavior, not real contamination. DIF capturing *any* temporal change (capability, methodology, RLHF) would produce orthogonality with contamination labels. This is useful but not a \"critique\" of contamination validation — it's a demonstration that the methods measure different constructs.\n4. **(Minor) \"Diagnostic lens\" framing feels post-hoc.** The difficulty×direction interaction and model-family profiles are interesting descriptives, but \"diagnostic lens\" implies more actionability than demonstrated. What should maintainers *do* with the information?\n5. **(Minor) Scope diffuse.** Three contributions could each be separate focused papers; together, none gets sufficient depth.\n6. **(Minor) Concurrent work overlap with Growing Pains (Habba et al. 2026).** Both use psychometric methods on temporal comparability with METABENCH-like data. Delineation (DIF vs FPC) is speculative without empirical demonstration.\n7. **(Minor) Reference count thin in main body beyond Related Work section.**\n\n**Questions to Authors:**\n1. Have you considered that DIF primarily captures capability change? If so, why frame as \"meta-critique\" rather than evidence that the methods simply measure different things?\n2. Have you tested more subtle injection patterns (partial flipping preserving difficulty ordering)?\n3. Mechanistic hypothesis for difficulty×direction interaction (ρ = −0.597)?\n4. How sensitive to temporal boundary? Sliding-window analysis tried?\n5. Given 24.8% additional non-uniform DIF from logistic regression, why not use it as primary method?\n6. Do item features associated with DIF here match those in Maeda (2025)?\n\n**R2 Scores:** Soundness 3.5 | Excitement 2.5 | Reproducibility 4.0 | Overall 3.0 | Confidence 4\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Sharing Summary\n\nReviews were exchanged. Key observations from cross-reading:\n\n**R1 on R2's review:** Agrees that related work gaps are valid and the circularity concern about the meta-critique is well-taken. However, considers R2's excitement score (2.5) slightly too low — the ground truth meta-critique alone is a non-trivial insight even if the core method is standard. Notes that R2 doesn't raise the model non-independence issue, which R1 considers the most technically consequential concern.\n\n**R2 on R1's review:** Agrees the model non-independence concern (R1-W3) is an important technical issue not identified in R2's own review. Considers R1's excitement score (3.0) slightly generous given the off-the-shelf methodology. Notes R1's question about matched-ability power reduction is incisive and could undermine the claimed 2.4pp confound estimate.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Convergence\n\n### Areas of Full Agreement\n1. **Scale and controls are impressive.** Both reviewers find the empirical work thorough and the multiple control conditions convincing.\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique is the strongest contribution.** Both identify the global-vs-differential distinction as the paper's best intellectual contribution.\n3. **Core method is standard, limiting novelty.** Both agree MH-DIF is applied without modification and this constrains the methodological contribution claim.\n4. **Semi-synthetic framework is competent but not deeply novel.** Both note psychometric precedent exists.\n5. **Reproducibility is strong.** Public data, standard methods, sufficient detail. Both score 4/5.\n6. **Honest limitations handling.** Both credit the paper for intellectual honesty.\n7. **Soundness is good but not flawless.** Both score 3.5/5, identifying real but non-fatal methodological concerns.\n\n### Areas of Partial Disagreement\n1. **Excitement/Overall gap (3.0–3.5 vs 2.5–3.0).** R1 rates the paper slightly higher, weighting the empirical thoroughness and the meta-critique insight more. R2 weights novelty more heavily and penalizes the off-the-shelf methodology and diffuse scope.\n2. **Severity of causal interpretation gap.** R1 treats it as the primary weakness; R2 treats it as a component of the circularity problem. Both agree it limits actionability.\n3. **Model non-independence.** R1 raises this as a major concern; R2 did not initially but acknowledges its importance upon cross-reading.\n\n### Resolved During Discussion\n- Both agree the model non-independence issue is technically valid and should be flagged to authors.\n- Both agree related work gaps (R2-W2) need addressing regardless of whether they affect the score.\n- Both agree the \"diagnostic lens\" contribution is the weakest of the three and could be folded into Discussion rather than being a numbered contribution.\n\n### Convergent Overall Assessment\nBoth reviewers converge on the paper being a solid empirical study with a valuable insight (global-vs-differential) but limited by: (a) no methodological novelty beyond application context, (b) unresolved causal interpretation, (c) model non-independence concern, (d) related work gaps. The disagreement is whether this reaches borderline conference (3.5) or clear Findings (3.0).\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Final Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n|-----------|----|----|-----------|\n| Soundness | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3.5** |\n| Excitement | 3.0 | 2.5 | **3.0** |\n| Reproducibility | 4.0 | 4.0 | **4.0** |\n| Overall | 3.5 | 3.0 | **3.0** |\n| Confidence | 4 | 4 | **4** |\n\n**Consensus Overall: 3.0 (Findings)**\n\n### Rationale\n\nThe paper makes a genuine empirical contribution: the 31% temporal DIF finding is well-established through multiple controls, the global-vs-differential distinction is intellectually valuable, and the semi-synthetic validation framework is carefully designed. The scale (5,227 models × 6 benchmarks) is unprecedented for DIF analysis in LLM evaluation.\n\nHowever, three factors prevent main conference acceptance:\n\n1. **Limited methodological novelty.** MH-DIF is applied unchanged from Holland & Thayer (1988). Cross-subject matching has psychometric precedent. The contribution is in the application domain, not the technique — a profile more natural for Findings than Main.\n\n2. **Unresolved causal interpretation.** The 31% DIF rate conflates contamination, capability improvement, training methodology shifts, and format sensitivity. Without decomposition, the practical implications (the audit protocol) remain speculative. The \"diagnostic lens\" framing acknowledges this but doesn't resolve it.\n\n3. **Model non-independence.** The 5,227 models are not independent examinees — they share base weights, training data, and architecture families. This fundamental assumption violation is not addressed beyond graduated deduplication (which reduces C% to 22.7–29.1% but doesn't formally test the model-correlation structure's impact on MH statistics).\n\nThe ground truth meta-critique is the paper's most distinctive contribution and would benefit from deeper development — engaging more directly with multiple contamination detection methods rather than arguing by transitivity from web-overlap labels.\n\n### Key Revision Recommendations (if resubmitting)\n\n1. **Address model non-independence formally.** Either (a) run permutations that shuffle group labels while preserving model family structure, or (b) restrict analysis to independent model families and show the finding holds. This is the most consequential methodological gap.\n\n2. **Expand related work.** Add Deng et al. (2024), Yang et al. (2023), Jacovi et al. (2023), Raji et al. (2021). Engage substantively with Maeda (2025) and Growing Pains (Habba et al. 2026) — the former for DIF feature comparison, the latter for methodology delineation (DIF vs FPC).\n\n3. **Develop the causal decomposition.** The difficulty×direction interaction (ρ = −0.597) is a promising lead. A structured analysis decomposing DIF into contamination-driven vs capability-driven components (even if only partially successful) would significantly strengthen the paper.\n\n4. **Tighten scope.** Consider dropping \"diagnostic lens\" as a numbered contribution and folding it into Discussion. Two sharp contributions (empirical finding + validation framework with meta-critique) > three diffuse ones.\n\n5. **Clarify FPR terminology.** The baseline \"FPR\" of 0.314 in semi-synthetic tables is the genuine temporal DIF rate, not false positives. Relabel to avoid confusion.\n\n6. **Downweight GSM1K analysis.** Move to appendix or shorten considerably — N = 33 with wide CIs does not support the prominence given in the current text.\n\n### Strengths to Preserve\n- The thoroughness of empirical controls is exemplary and should not be reduced\n- The global-vs-differential distinction is a lasting conceptual contribution\n- The semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching, while not deeply novel, is a reusable methodological tool\n- The honest, non-sensationalized framing (\"instability ≠ invalidity\") builds credibility","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260521T165710","overall_score":"3.0 — Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1498,"cache_read_input_tokens":69484,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1498},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"323cfb50-fdc2-4808-8997-11b497c1cdb0","uuid":"66e6f9fc-bb8e-4909-9eed-179b5745a75d"}
